WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.820
- The FBI raided Trump's
house in Mar-a-Lago.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.250
Well, the FBI executed a search warrant

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.020
and took a bunch of documents and things.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.980
But yes, this is unprecedented in America.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.470
Yes, it could be the beginning
of a criminal prosecution

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.000
of the former president.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.040
Yes, it could be the tip of the spear

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.560
of a political witch hunt
by the Biden administration

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.630
against a political rival.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.580
Or it could be a mundane effort

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.080
to retrieve archival documents

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.300
that leads to absolutely nothing else.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.590
We just don't know.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.930
- The FBI, and I give them a lot of credit

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.490
because they're fighting forces

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.200
that they're not supposed to be fighting.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.120
- But what do we know
about the search warrant

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.610
that was carried out against
former President Trump?

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.650
We can't make many
assumptions about the FBI raid

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.750
until we see the actual warrant or until,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.400
you know, we see an indictment.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.780
But here's what we do know.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.430
The Justice Department is conducting

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.520
at least two investigations
that we know of into Trump.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.830
The DOJ is looking at his
attempt to overturn the result

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.430
of the 2020 presidential election
and is also investigating

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.620
the alleged mishandling
of classified records.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.540
The National Archive is
charged with collecting

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.220
and sorting presidential material.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.050
The National Archives has publicly stated

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.970
that it had to recover at least 15 boxes

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.770
of White House records that Trump took

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.840
to his Mar-a-Lago resort.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.450
The records include some
classified material.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.740
And we know that Trump has a history

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.480
of destroying and withholding records.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.580
He was repeatedly warned
about destroying documents,

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.190
but according to CNN, "Politico,"
and "The Washington Post,"

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.340
he tore them up and threw them
in the trash or on the floor.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.900
White House officials reassembled

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.640
some of those records with tape.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.960
The National Archives confirmed

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.970
that the Trump administration
sent the archives

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.340
some documents that had
been taped back together

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.860
as well as records that
were still in shreds.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:35.910
I'll be doing a separate video

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.230
on the Presidential Records Act

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.240
and the implications of the Espionage Act,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.510
but multiple sources told
CNN that in April and May,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.850
the FBI interviewed
Trump's Mar-a-Lago aides,

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.910
and in June of 2022,
investigators visited Mar-a-Lago

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.110
to discuss Trump's removal

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.850
of potentially classified material

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.830
that had been taken to
the Florida property.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.480
One of those investigators, Jay Bratt,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.460
is the chief of the Counterintelligence

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.070
and Export Control Section
at the Justice Department.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.690
Trump reportedly greeted the investigators

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:01.890
but did not answer questions.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.360
The investigators asked to see

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.010
where the documents were being stored.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.230
Trump's lawyers showed
them a room in the basement

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:08.970
where boxes of records were being stored.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.160
Some reports said that
the documents were marked

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.690
with TOP SECRET.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.820
On June 8th, the
investigators sent a letter

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.920
to Trump's lawyers telling
them to secure the room

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.450
where the secret documents were stored.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.130
Trump aides complied with the request

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.750
and added a padlock to the door.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.790
Now, I probably don't need to
tell you just how insane it is

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.250
that there could be top-secret
classified documents

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.800
being stored in some
basement in Mar-a-Lago

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.800
or just how insane it is that
the FBI raided Mar-a-Lago

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.790
potentially over these documents.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.180
But I do have to say that
"they even broke into my safe"

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.660
is probably going to go down
as a quote for the ages.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.820
But the search began early Monday morning

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.740
and apparently focused
on the area of the club

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.380
where Trump's offices and
personal quarters are located.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.660
The investigators met with
two of Trump's attorneys

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.970
and Trump reportedly
greeted the investigators

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.620
but did not answer questions.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.410
Investigators removed more
than a dozen boxes of items.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.757
Eric Trump told Fox News
that he was told, quote,

00:03:00.757 --> 00:03:03.300
"the purpose of the raid,
from what they said,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.280
was because the National
Archives wanted to, you know,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.950
corroborate whether or not
Donald Trump had any documents

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:08.850
in his possession."

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:09.990
Trump's lawyer, Christina Bobb,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.250
said the FBI seized documents

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.050
and Trump cooperated with the FBI.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.670
Quote: "President Trump and his legal team

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.160
have been cooperative with
the FBI and DOJ officials

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.330
every step of the way.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.780
The FBI did conduct an unannounced
raid and seized paper."

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.550
Although Bobb said the
raid was unannounced,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.950
CNN reported the FBI and
Secret Service communicated

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.300
about the raid before it happened

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.560
to ensure that it went smoothly.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.540
Donald Trump was in New
York on the day of the raid,

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.330
and the Biden administration
was reportedly not aware

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.890
of the raid, according to CNN.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.930
Now, we can assume this
raid did not happen

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.570
at the behest of a rogue FBI agent

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.910
or a local judge acting without buy-in

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.760
or notification of
high-level DOJ officials.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.070
Obviously serving a warrant
on a former president

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.560
is an incredibly serious
thing, and it's a decision

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.700
that can't be taken lightly.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.950
The FBI and DOJ must follow constitutional

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.080
and statutory requirements
for obtaining a warrant,

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.580
which we'll talk about in a moment,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.950
but in addition, the DOJ has guidelines

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.380
for investigations of political targets.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.690
In 2020, former attorney
general William Barr

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:04.890
issued a controversial memorandum

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.600
stating that anyone in
the Justice Department

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.780
who was investigating a
political candidate must run it

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.820
by the attorney general for approval,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.680
and current attorney
general Merrick Garland

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.350
renewed that memo on May 25th, 2022.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.990
Garland's memo is titled
"Election Year Sensitivities."

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.610
The memo reminded DOJ employees

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.290
of the department's long-standing policy

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.600
for avoiding steps that could
be perceived as partisan

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.660
and designed to influence
an election result.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.510
Quote: "This is particularly
important in an election year.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.090
Now that the 2022 election
season is upon us,

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.330
and as in prior election cycles,
I am using this memorandum

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.430
to remind you of the
department's existing policies

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.110
with respect to political activities."

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.090
Now, Barr's original
memo says investigations,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.610
including preliminary
ones, into presidential

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.040
or vice presidential
candidates, their campaigns,

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.650
or staff cannot be opened
without the written approval

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:50.970
of the attorney general.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.650
It also states that an investigation

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:53.850
into a congressional candidate

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.410
or campaign cannot be opened

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.780
without first notifying the
assistant attorney general

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.240
and the respective US attorney
in the district involved,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.860
and if an investigation is initiated

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.180
in line with these guidelines,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.240
then the investigating agency
has to provide regular updates

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.100
to the deputy attorney general.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.320
Now, Trump has indicated
that he will be a candidate

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.150
for president in 2022.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.470
So the TL;DR version here

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.820
is that the FBI would not have applied

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.740
for a search warrant
without Garland's consent,

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.020
and presumably, they also
would've gotten approval

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.600
from the Trump-appointed FBI
director, Christopher Wray.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.390
But what is the requirement
for a federal search warrant?

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.580
Well, a search warrant
authorizes law enforcement

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.410
to search a location for
any of the following:

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.530
evidence of a crime,
contraband, fruits of crime,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.020
or other items illegally possessed,

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.360
property designed for
use, intended for use,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.410
or used in committing a crime,
or a person to be arrested

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.930
or a person who is unlawfully restrained.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.030
Now, usually those first
three are lumped together.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.700
Imagine a simple burglary
where jewelry was stolen.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.350
You'd want to look at the burglar's house

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.120
for the stolen jewelry,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.100
cash or drugs that might have been traded

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.830
for the stolen jewelry, or burglary tools.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.860
And so law enforcement has to
identify two different things:

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.540
one, the person or
property to be searched,

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.340
and two, the items to be seized.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.630
Now, more to the point,

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.820
law enforcement has to
establish probable cause

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.400
to believe that the
items to be seized exist

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.950
and will be located in the person

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.080
or property to be identified,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.510
and probable cause is hard to define.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:19.260
It's imprecise, fluid, and
very dependent on context.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:20.580
The US Supreme Court says that finding

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.357
whether probable cause exists is, quote,

00:06:22.357 --> 00:06:24.810
"a practical, common-sense
decision whether,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.230
given all the circumstances,
there is a fair probability

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.510
that contraband or evidence
of a crime will be found

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.800
in a particular place."

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.830
And what does fair
probability actually mean?

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.230
Well, it doesn't have a
specific number attached to it,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.970
unlike the standard of
preponderance of the evidence,

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.520
which is just a hair over 50% or more.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.320
That said, several
studies over the decades

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.330
that involved surveys of
judges generally agree

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.560
that probable cause is somewhere

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.470
in the high 40s but beneath 50%.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:50.303
And it might be worth noting

00:06:50.303 --> 00:06:52.410
that some jurisdictions,
like Oregon, have collapsed

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.750
the probable cause standard
and the preponderance standard

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:58.920
so that probable cause means
anything over 50% likelihood.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.070
But that is not the federal
or constitutional standard.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.950
The constitutional standard
is only a fair probability.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:05.783
Then you might ask,

00:07:05.783 --> 00:07:08.820
how specific does a federal
search warrant have to be?

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.280
Well, generally speaking,
not that specific.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.530
We can look to the search
warrant that was used

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:14.640
against Paul Manafort.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.627
It includes something
very specific, quote,

00:07:16.627 --> 00:07:20.400
"a Bijan Black Titanium Royal Way watch,"

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.380
but it also includes
general categories of items,

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.920
like "any and all financial
records for Paul Manafort, Jr."

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.470
Now, we'll get into what
that means in practice later,

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:29.910
but let's talk about the practice

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.830
for getting a federal search warrant.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.990
Now, the applicant for a search warrant

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.600
is generally a federal law
enforcement officer of some kind,

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.020
an FBI agent, a Treasury agent, et cetera,

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.780
and as of 2016, federal agencies employed

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.080
about 132,000 full-time
law enforcement officers.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.910
And every federal search
warrant is reviewed

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.800
by an assistant US attorney of some kind.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:53.490
Now, that's not required by
law, but it's a policy decision

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.870
by the Department of Justice.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:57.120
Now, the application itself
is a one-page template,

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.640
and federal law enforcement
generally attaches

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:01.740
three documents to that application.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.620
The first is the affidavit
by the investigating officer,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.170
attachment A includes the
property to be searched,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.110
and attachment B lists
the items to be seized.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:12.000
Now, the affidavit is a sworn declaration

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.980
by the law enforcement
officer that sets forth

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.470
the officer's knowledge
of the investigation.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.630
It can include hearsay
and even information

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:21.030
from anonymous or confidential sources,

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.500
though the officer needs to establish

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.870
the reliability of that information.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.930
The purpose of that affidavit
is to establish probable cause

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.280
to the reviewing magistrate.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:31.440
But the affidavit is all there is.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.090
The magistrate does not get to consider

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.430
any additional information,
just what's contained

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.710
within the four corners of that document.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.030
Now, that said, the magistrate judge

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.640
has to issue the warrant
if there is probable cause.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.230
It's not optional.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.007
The judge does not have discretion to say,

00:08:45.007 --> 00:08:47.250
"Well, there is probably probable cause,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:48.900
but I don't think you need
it for your investigation.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.940
It's looking pretty good on its own."

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.010
Once the magistrate judge
has found probable cause,

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:53.970
the warrant must issue.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.170
And what this means in practice

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.840
is that the magistrate
judge signs the application,

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.170
the one-page template, and
issues it to another officer

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:02.670
authorized to execute it.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:05.220
The warrant must be
executed within 14 days

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.140
and during the daytime,
unless there's good cause

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.970
to execute it at some other time.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:09.960
And this should go without saying,

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.120
but I'm gonna say it again anyway:

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.310
Just because a judge signs
off on it doesn't mean

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.720
a crime has been committed,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.660
and just because you have
received a federal search warrant

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.730
doesn't mean that you are a criminal

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:23.130
or have done really anything wrong,

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.130
see contra all of the
legal bad takes on Twitter.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.720
And as a reminder,

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.670
just because a search warrant was executed

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.250
on a particular person's
property, that doesn't mean

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:34.800
that particular person is the
target of an investigation.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.260
Now, it is very common
for that to be the case,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.150
but search warrants are frequently served

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.730
on Google and Meta for
information and data

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.020
that they keep on their servers

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.060
that are relevant to an investigation.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.430
They're not the target
of that investigation.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.260
Now, it's rare, but occasionally,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.060
search warrants are executed on members

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.250
of the media who have
information or lawyers

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.310
who represent the targets
of an investigation.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:58.890
Nor does it mean there
absolutely will be a prosecution.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:01.440
Just because there is
probable cause doesn't mean

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.790
that the government can prove a case

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.310
beyond a reasonable doubt in a jury trial.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.200
That's a completely separate consideration

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:08.640
in light of all the evidence,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.070
including whatever evidence comes out

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.660
as a result of the search warrant.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.940
The beyond-a-reasonable-doubt
standard is much,

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.070
much higher than the probable cause

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:18.900
or even the preponderance
of the evidence standard,

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.310
though it's always good to remind people

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:21.750
that just because you aren't convicted

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.100
beyond a reasonable doubt doesn't mean

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:25.020
you didn't commit a crime also.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.220
You know, so it goes both ways.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.260
Now, there are some
differences between state

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.690
and federal courts when it
comes to search warrants.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.540
For one, the states don't have
a single uniform procedure

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:34.710
like the federal courts do.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.540
Now, they all basically
get you to the same place,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.100
but they certainly won't look as uniform

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.570
as they do in the federal system.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.970
And state judges have a reputation

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.510
for rubber-stamping applications
for search warrants.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.240
The federal system has
the Department of Justice

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.220
that acts as a gatekeeper
for search warrants,

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.500
even though that's not
required by federal law,

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.450
so state judges are more
likely to get warrants

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.360
that contain plenty of
grammatical and formatting issues

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.510
and might not have quite as much evidence

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.100
as far as probable cause goes.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.720
But in any event, the end result

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.030
is that officers will
show up to a location,

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.830
knocking on the door,
notifying the people inside

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:10.320
of the search warrant, and
then searching the scene.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.000
That means getting
everyone out of the place

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.910
so that they can't tamper with
evidence or present a threat.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.850
The officers then search
the place in accordance

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:19.410
with what's allowed in the warrant.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.570
They can search for the items authorized

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.280
by the warrant and open any containers

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.200
that could reasonably contain the items,

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.960
including safes, lockboxes, and the like.

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.960
And using the Paul Manafort
search warrant as an example,

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.947
we saw that there was
a pretty big category,

00:11:32.947 --> 00:11:35.880
"any and all financial records
for Paul Manafort, Jr."

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.220
So let's say that you're one
of the FBI agents on the scene.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:40.470
You would basically look
for documents and papers.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:42.270
And let's say that you
find a manila folder

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.310
that contains 100 or so pages.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:47.160
You might skim through it, and as you do,

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:48.660
you see a couple of financial records

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.850
that pertain to Paul Manafort.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.310
You would then likely seize
the entire manila folder.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:54.270
There are two reasons for this.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.030
One, people typically
keep financial records

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.460
with other financial
records or related records

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.880
and those related records
might be needed to make sense

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.460
of the specific financial
records that they're kept with,

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.770
and second, you don't
have time at the scene

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:11.010
to fully understand how all
the documents link together.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.810
In other words, you don't
understand the import

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:14.160
of those documents at the scene.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.960
So you'd probably take
the entire manila folder

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:18.510
and notate on an inventory form something

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.150
like "manila folder
containing financial documents

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.720
located in the lower-right-hand
desk drawer in the office."

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.300
Now, agents keep an inventory
of what items they seize

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.190
and where they found those items.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.380
They are required by law to do this.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.240
When they're done executing
the search warrant,

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.190
they will leave a copy
of the search warrant

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:38.100
as well as a copy of the
inventory of things seized.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:41.340
Now, it's important to note
they do not leave a copy

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:42.570
of the affidavit.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:44.490
All they will leave is
the one-page application,

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.590
along with the two attachments
listing the property

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:49.020
to be searched and the items to be seized.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:50.370
After that, the investigating officers

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.500
would then return the search
warrant to the magistrate,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.270
and it would specify the date and time

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:55.230
the warrant was executed

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.330
and include the inventory of items seized.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:58.290
So what's next?

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.360
Well, Trump has a copy
of the search warrant

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.160
but not the supporting affidavit.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.990
He could release it if he chose to.

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:07.020
There's no legal reason
why he couldn't release it,

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.810
and it's interesting that
he has chosen not to.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.480
And additionally, there are
reports of a mole or informant

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.750
inside of Mar-a-Lago, which
might explain the exigency

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.690
of the FBI's search of Mar-a-Lago.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.520
Additionally, "The Florida Times-Union,"

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.470
Judicial Watch, and "The New York Times"

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:24.660
have filed a motion to unseal the records.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:26.550
The supporting affidavit is sealed

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.740
to protect the
investigation, and generally,

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:31.950
the DOJ doesn't comment
on ongoing investigations

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.750
because it could jeopardize
the investigation

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:35.100
and tip off other targets

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:38.220
or the target who is the initial target.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.810
- [French Narrator] 12 seconds later.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.330
- Except, of course, when the DOJ chooses

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:43.710
that it's going to make a statement

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:45.450
about an ongoing investigation,

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:48.240
like when you're trying to get
a friggin' YouTube video out

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.730
and the attorney general for
the United States chooses

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:53.853
to give a press confidence. (yells)

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.730
Attorney general Merrick Garland confirmed

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.140
in a press conference that indeed,

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:00.120
he did have personal supervision

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.860
over the search warrant that was executed

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:05.040
over the former president's
estate at Mar-a-Lago.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:06.900
And in fact, because
former President Trump

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:09.270
did confirm the search
warrant was executed

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.850
and publicly stated information about it,

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.640
the DOJ is going to move
to unseal the records

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:17.310
related to the search warrant itself.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:19.500
One gets the sense that the DOJ was trying

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:22.110
to keep this quiet, and in
fact, this comes on the heels

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:23.820
of a report from "The New York Times"

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:28.140
that a subpoena was issued
against former President Trump

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:31.530
to return some or all of
the documents in question

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.570
and that may have not been complied with,

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.790
which might have forced the DOJ's hand

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.430
to execute a search warrant
to get these documents.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.620
And now we're facing the
incredibly interesting situation

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.180
where it's the DOJ that is moving

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:44.340
to disclose this information

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:45.960
and it may be former President Trump

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:47.400
who doesn't want it disclosed.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:48.810
He, of course, always had the ability

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:52.440
to disclose the receipt
and the other pages

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:56.610
of the warrant other than
the probable cause affidavit,

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.830
and now he may be in a situation

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.560
where he wants to keep that secret.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:02.430
But at least that's likely

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:04.410
to be the last bombshell
that we have to deal with

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:06.270
in this particular case for a while.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:07.860
- [French Narrator] 12 seconds later.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.170
- Are you freaking kidding me?

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.420
So "The Washington Post" is now reporting

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.440
that the documents at issue
here concern nuclear weapons,

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.000
which explains a whole lot

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.960
about why this search
warrant was executed,

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:24.600
why the exigent circumstances,

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:26.820
and why everyone is freaking out.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:28.590
It also throws a huge curveball

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:29.580
in the people that were saying

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:31.950
that the president could
just declassify documents

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:33.600
on the way out of the White House.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.040
When it comes to nuclear
weapons-related documents, often,

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.530
that has to go through a
separate regulatory agency.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:43.530
Often, the president is in
charge of declassification,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.480
but when it comes to
these kinds of documents,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.210
that is not necessarily the case.

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:49.800
It also raises all kinds of questions

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.980
as to why former President
Trump took these documents

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:56.070
out of the White House and
why he might have refused

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:59.730
to turn them back over when
multiple requests were made,

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:01.800
which is what has been alleged.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:05.040
So we'll cover all of the
potential criminal implications

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:06.930
of this in a separate video,

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.990
but this whole situation is
raising all kinds of questions.

